1. Physical Wave Propagation
Sound originates as a pressure disturbance moving through a medium. This physical propagation is defined by the 3D Wave Equation:

 * u: Wave displacement/pressure.
 * t: Time.
 * c: Speed of sound in the medium.
 * \nabla^2: Laplacian operator describing spatial curvature.
Link: To analyze this continuous physical wave, it must be decomposed into its constituent harmonic frequencies.
2. Frequency Decomposition and Harmonics
A complex wave is isolated into discrete frequency components via the Fourier Series:

 * \omega_0: Fundamental angular frequency.
 * a_n, b_n: Amplitudes of the harmonics.
These components follow a strict integer multiple relationship defined as the Harmonic Series:

Step: Given f_1 = 200\text{ Hz}, the second harmonic is f_2 = 2(200) = 400\text{ Hz}.
Resonance occurs when a system is driven at its natural frequency, maximizing amplitude:

Link: To process these continuous frequencies computationally, the signal must be converted into a discrete digital format without losing frequency information.
3. Digital Sampling and Aliasing Limits
The Nyquist-Shannon Sampling Theorem dictates the minimum sampling rate (f_s) required to accurately capture a continuous signal with a maximum bandwidth (B):

Failure to meet this condition results in aliasing. The frequency at which an undersampled signal appears (f_{\text{alias}}) is calculated by folding it around the Nyquist frequency:

 * k: Integer multiple bringing the frequency into the Nyquist range.
Step-by-Step Aliasing Calculation:
 * Sample Rate: f_s = 44,100\text{ Hz}.
 * Nyquist Limit: f_N = 44,100 / 2 = 22,050\text{ Hz}.
 * Input Signal: f = 30,000\text{ Hz}.
 * Condition Check: 30,000 > 22,050 (Aliasing will occur).
 * Calculation: f_{\text{alias}} = \vert{}30,000 - 44,100\vert{} = \vert{}-14,100\vert{} = 14,100\text{ Hz}.
 * Result: A 30,000 Hz tone folds back as a false 14,100 Hz component.
Link: While standard digital sampling captures frequency content, it discards temporal location. We require a mathematical method to locate when specific frequencies occur.
4. Time-Frequency Localization
The Short-Time Fourier Transform (STFT) introduces a sliding window w[n-m] to isolate segments of the discrete signal x[n]:

 * m: Time position index.
 * w: Window function (e.g., Hann or Hamming) applied to reduce spectral leakage at boundaries.
This operation is strictly bound by the Gabor limit (uncertainty principle for signal processing), enforcing an inverse relationship between time resolution (\Delta t) and frequency resolution (\Delta f):

 * Increasing window length improves \Delta f but degrades \Delta t.
 * Decreasing window length improves \Delta t but degrades \Delta f.
Link: The resulting STFT spectrogram maps absolute acoustic energy, but it does not map to how biological systems interpret those frequencies.
5. Psychoacoustic Scaling
Human perception is logarithmic, requiring non-linear transformations of the STFT output.
Loudness:
Perceived loudness S (in sones) scales exponentially relative to sound pressure level L_N (in phons):

 * Step: If L_N increases from 40 to 50 phons:
   S_1 = 2^{(40-40)/10} = 2^0 = 1 sone.
   S_2 = 2^{(50-40)/10} = 2^1 = 2 sones.
 * Result: A 10-phon increase results in an exact mathematical doubling of perceived loudness.
Pitch (Missing Fundamental):
The auditory system reconstructs fundamental frequencies based on harmonic spacing, using the greatest common divisor:

 * Step: Given isolated tones at 400 Hz, 600 Hz, and 800 Hz. \gcd(400, 600, 800) = 200\text{ Hz}.
 * Result: The perceived pitch is 200 Hz.
Link: Applying these perceptual rules (e.g., Mel-scale filter banks, log-compression) to the STFT yields Mel-Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCCs) and Mel Spectrograms, which serve as the numerical inputs for machine learning architectures.
6. Sequence Modeling and Attention (Audio AI)
Audio information is sequentially dependent. Modern processing shifts from local convolutional windows to global contextual relationships via the self-attention mechanism:

 * Q (Queries), K (Keys), V (Values): Projections of the flattened spectrogram patches.
 * d_k: Scaling factor to stabilize gradients.
This operation computes the dot product of every time-frequency patch against all others, mathematically determining the relevance (weight) of acoustic events regardless of their temporal separation.
